## 10. Scaling Amazon RDS

### A. Vertical Scaling

Vertical scaling means changing the DB instance class to provide more compute resources.

For example:

```text
Smaller DB Instance
        |
        | Modify instance class
        v
Larger DB Instance
```

Use this when CPU or memory resources are insufficient. A modification can cause downtime or a brief interruption depending on the configuration.

### B. Storage Scaling

For supported storage configurations, RDS can increase allocated storage automatically when free storage reaches configured thresholds.

Configure maximum storage limits and monitor growth to reduce the risk of running out of disk space.

### C. Read Scaling

Read replicas can distribute read-heavy workloads across multiple database instances.

### D. Connection Management

Applications should use connection pooling where appropriate. Too many database connections can consume resources and reduce performance.

For supported architectures, Amazon RDS Proxy can help pool and manage database connections, improving application scalability and resilience.

