## 11. Amazon RDS vs. Database on EC2

| Feature | Amazon RDS | Database on EC2 |
|---|---|---|
| Database installation | Mostly managed by AWS | Managed by the user |
| OS administration | AWS-managed | User-managed |
| Backups | Managed backup features available | User designs backup process |
| Patching | Managed according to RDS settings | User manages OS and database patching |
| Customization | More restricted | Greater control |
| Operational effort | Usually lower | Usually higher |
| Best suited for | Standard relational workloads | Special configurations and full control |

### When Should You Choose RDS?

Choose RDS when you want a managed relational database and prefer to reduce routine administration.

Choose a self-managed database on EC2 when you require operating-system access, custom database configurations, or capabilities not supported by RDS.

---

## 12. Amazon RDS vs. Amazon DynamoDB

| Feature | Amazon RDS | Amazon DynamoDB |
|---|---|---|
| Database type | Relational | NoSQL key-value and document database |
| Data model | Tables with rows and columns | Items and attributes |
| Querying | SQL | API-based queries and supported expressions |
| Relationships | Supports relational joins | Different data-modelling approach |
| Scaling | Instance/storage scaling and read replicas, depending on engine | Managed scaling options |
| Typical use cases | Business applications, transactions, relational data | High-scale key-value access, serverless applications |

**Example:** Use RDS when your application needs relational data and SQL queries. Consider DynamoDB when your access patterns suit its key-value/document model and managed scaling capabilities.

